(Process-control leaves: directories, symbolic links, mount/unmount,
 fork/reap, the reaped status, its decoders, `Execute` and `Send signal`.
 docs/ledger/process-control.md, kinds 260-269, the first slots of the
 new 260-279 "process" reserved block (see the reservation table above
 'gen dispatch leaf' in gen_core.vox, which the master extends at merge).

 THE GENERATION POLICY THIS FILE ENCODES (surface brief
 vf-leaves-proca, night 2026-08-29->30; cf. PROCEDURE.md sect.6's
 pre-existing "Process-control and privileged syscalls" carve-out,
 Josj 2026-08-20):

 - `Mount`/`Unmount`, `Reboot` and `Shutdown` are SAFE TO EMIT AS-IS: the
   campaign always runs unprivileged, so the kernel refuses every one of
   them with EPERM before touching anything, and asserting the refusal
   is itself a real memory-safety-relevant test of the syscall codegen
   and the error-flag path. Hand-verified this session against both
   pinned compilers: real reboot(2)/mount(2) calls, the machine still
   here, execution continuing past all of them (see the report).
   `Poweroff`/`Restart`/`Halt` and their aliases are NOT part of this
   ruling - the brief names only `mount`, `reboot` and `shutdown` - and
   stay out of scope, and out of tests/200_never_emitted.vox's guard,
   pending a wider one.
 - `Execute` draws its program from a FIXED two-element allowlist,
   `/usr/bin/true` and `/usr/bin/false`, and nothing else, ever - never a
   generated command line, never a path built from a variable.
 - `Send signal` sends ONLY signal 0 (the existence check, which
   delivers nothing), and ONLY to the pid a `fork` in the SAME leaf just
   returned - never a drawn number, never a reserved-invalid constant,
   never the program's own process group. The forked pid stays valid
   (running, or a zombie awaiting reap) until the same leaf reaps it, so
   the existence check always succeeds regardless of how fast the
   exec'd program finished.
 - Directory create/remove and symbolic links are Tier CONFINE
   (plan 323): every path lives under the program's own per-run scratch
   directory, `gen_scratch_flag` (gen_manifest.vox) - the same mechanism
   `src/gen_files.vox`'s file-I/O leaves already use, guarded by the
   same `gen_does_file_io`/`gen_scratch_flag` eligibility check, standing
   aside for a plain print leaf when the program drew no file I/O at
   all. Mount/Unmount are confined the same way even though an EPERM
   refusal never actually reaches the path.

 Two pieces of shared per-program state this file owns (reset once per
 program by gen_core.vox's 'gen program', alongside gen_does_file_io and
 friends - see the reset lines there):

 - gen_process_decoders_declared: the manual's own `'exit code of'`/
   `'signal of'` functions (LANGUAGE.md:4423-4427) can be declared only
   ONCE per program - a second `To 'exit code of' ...` is a duplicate-
   label NASM assembly error, hand-verified this session (see the
   report) - and kind 266 can be drawn more than once in a program with
   budget > 1, since every kind in this file is top-level-only and
   therefore redrawable at every remaining top-level statement slot.
 - gen_process_has_reaped: PRC-58's `-1` sentinel is only true BEFORE
   the first successful reap anywhere in the whole program, and five
   different kinds in this file (264-268) can each perform one. Kind 265
   checks this flag before deciding whether it may still claim the
   sentinel value; every reaping kind sets it afterwards.

 Confinement and allowlisting are safety CONSTRUCTIONS, not language
 rules - CLAUDE.md's "three deliberate, declared invariants" the ledger
 already names for this section (docs/ledger/process-control.md,
 "Invariants this section justifies"). Everything else - which
 directory-remove spelling, which fork spelling, which of the two
 allowed programs, the exit code a forked child reports, the pid holder
 and companion names - is drawn, per PRC-07/08/43/72 and CLAUDE.md's
 "make everything random unless there is a rule to say otherwise".)

a boolean called gen_process_decoders_declared is false.
a boolean called gen_process_has_reaped is false.

(D21 (master chain, 2026-08-30): both flags above are per-PROGRAM state
 and must be reset every time 'gen program' runs, or a second call in the
 same process (exactly what tests/220_determinism.vox and
 tests/270_layout.vox both do) inherits the first call's leftover values -
 kind 266 silently stops declaring the decoder functions and kind 265
 silently stops claiming the -1 sentinel on the SAME seed's second run,
 which is a real behaviour difference, not layout noise. Neither flag is
 draw-dependent (unlike the value-name cycle 'gen reset value names'
 restarts, which has to sit AFTER the reseed for exactly the opposite
 reason - see the comment above that call in gen_core.vox), so this reset
 belongs before it, grouped with the other plain per-program counters
 gen_core.vox resets at the top of 'gen program'.)
To 'gen reset process state'.
    Set gen_process_decoders_declared to false.
    Set gen_process_has_reaped to false.

(A generic per-program-unique identifier for a witness this file
 introduces - delegates to 'gen flow witness name' (gen_flow.vox) rather
 than calling 'gen buffer name'/'gen buffer reference' directly, per
 PROCEDURE.md sect.6a ("name allocators are generic - reuse, do not
 copy") and this surface brief's night-3 common brief. That indirection
 matters here, not just style: night-3's common brief records that a
 quoted ONE-WORD pool name (`'pantry'`) does not resolve inside a `{…}`
 format slot on 0.4.14, and every witness this file allocates is
 interpolated into one (an assertion message, an `On error Set {name}
 to true`) - hand-verified this session, compile-fails with "Unknown
 variable" exactly the way the brief predicts. 'gen flow witness name'
 is the existing fix: a one-word stem is returned bare, never quoted,
 since only the quoted spelling is broken; a multi-word stem still gets
 its closing quote from 'gen buffer reference' as normal.)
To 'gen process witness'.
    a text called 'the reference' is 'gen flow witness name'.
    Return a text, 'the reference'.

(An Exit code a forked child may safely report: wide and varied, but
 clear of 90-99, which PROCEDURE.md sect.6 reserves for the argv
 assertions (91-94) and the ledger's own wrong-value exits (95) -
 nothing generated may exit 90-99 for any other reason.)
To 'gen process safe exit code'.
    a number called 'the code' is 'rng below' of 90.
    Return a number, 'the code'.

(PRC-02, PRC-04: `Create a directory called '<path>'.` performs
 mkdir(2), mode 0755 (LANGUAGE.md:4241-4242; PRC-03's mode claim is not
 assertable - Vox has no stat/mode accessor). The leading article `a`
 is optional - both spellings are drawn, varying which. Confined to the
 per-run scratch directory; stands aside for a plain print leaf when
 the program drew no file I/O, the same eligibility gate
 'gen leaf file round trip' (gen_files.vox) uses.)
To 'gen leaf process directory create' with a text called indent and a boolean called nested.
    If gen_does_file_io is false then,
        'gen leaf print' of indent,
        Return a boolean, false.
    If gen_scratch_flag is empty then,
        Print "PROBE PRC-02: gen_does_file_io true but gen_scratch_flag unbound",
        'gen leaf print' of indent,
        Return a boolean, false.
    Set gen_file_ios to gen_file_ios add 1.
    a text called 'the path name' is 'gen process witness'.
    a number called 'the path number' is gen_buffer_names.
    a text called 'the path line' is "a text called {'the path name'} is \"{{{gen_scratch_flag}}}/procdir{'the path number'}\"".
    a number called 'the article draw' is 'rng below' of 2.
    a text called 'the create line' is "Create a directory called {'the path name'}".
    If 'the article draw' is 1 then,
        Set 'the create line' to "Create directory called {'the path name'}".
    a text called 'the assert line' is "If {'the path name'} is not available then, Print \"ASSERT PRC-02: expected available got missing\", Exit 95. Print \"created\"".
    a text called 'the cleanup line' is "Remove the directory called {'the path name'}".
    a list called 'the lines' is ['the path line', 'the create line', 'the assert line', 'the cleanup line'].
    'gen join lines' of indent and nested and 'the lines'.

(PRC-12, PRC-13, PRC-21: `Mount "<source>" at "<target>" with type
 "<fstype>" [with options "<options>"].` lowers directly to mount(2)
 (LANGUAGE.md:4261-4264); the `with options` clause is optional
 (LANGUAGE.md:4252, 4261); `Unmount "<target>".` lowers to umount2(2)
 (LANGUAGE.md:4271); both set the error flag on failure
 (LANGUAGE.md:4276). SAFE TO EMIT under this surface brief's ruling -
 see the file header - because the campaign is always unprivileged and
 mount(2)/umount2(2) refuse with EPERM before doing anything;
 hand-verified this session under both pinned compilers (see the
 report). Confined to the per-run scratch directory anyway, on the same
 belt-and-braces rule the file-I/O leaves use, even though an EPERM
 refusal never actually reaches the path; stands aside the same way as
 the two leaves above.)
