(PRC-72, PRC-73, PRC-75: `Send signal <N> to process <pid>.` performs
 kill(2) (LANGUAGE.md:4508-4509); `child` is an accepted alias for
 `process` (LANGUAGE.md:4510); signal 0 delivers nothing but errors only
 if no process has that pid, the standard existence check
 (LANGUAGE.md:4525-4526), and a successful send clears the error flag
 (LANGUAGE.md:4516). SAFETY (this surface brief): the target is ALWAYS
 signal 0, and ALWAYS the pid this leaf's own `fork` just returned -
 never a drawn number, never the process's own group. The pid stays
 valid (a live child, or a zombie awaiting reap) until this leaf reaps
 it, so the existence check always succeeds regardless of how fast the
 exec'd program finished. A foreign, deliberately-failing reap first
 sets the error flag, so the successful signal right after it proves
 PRC-73's "clears the flag" rather than merely "does not set a fresh
 one".)
