(PRC-11: all three directory statements - `Create`, `Remove`/`Delete`,
 `Change directory` - set the error flag on failure and `On error`
 catches it, execution continuing past all three (LANGUAGE.md:4246).
 Every failure here is engineered, not incidental, and stays inside the
 program's own scratch directory throughout: the create targets a path
 whose PARENT does not exist (mkdir(2) refuses ENOENT without the real
 filesystem outside scratch ever being touched); the remove/delete and
 the change-directory both target that same never-created path, so
 change-directory's failure is the safe way to exercise the construct at
 all - chdir(2) leaves the calling process's cwd untouched on failure,
 hand-verified this session under both pinned compilers, so this leaf
 can never do to a sibling leaf in the same generated program what
 PRC-10's own ledger row warns a SUCCESSFUL chdir would (move the cwd out
 from under every gen_scratch_flag-relative path built afterwards) -
 which is also why this leaf never attempts a successful chdir at all.
 (The ledger's own retained probe, docs/ledger/probes/process-control/
 PRC-11.vox, forces the CREATE failure with a real /proc path instead;
 this leaf stays scratch-confined throughout instead, per this file's
 Tier CONFINE convention, and still proves the identical claim.) Stands
 aside for a plain print leaf when the program drew no file I/O, the
 same eligibility gate the other confined leaves in this file use.)
