(HELD OUT, kind 352, unregistered - candidate found this batch, reported
 in full in REPORT-LEAVES-PROCESS-B.md: under RANDOM layout, seed 1006
 (budget 12) puts a single newline between a reap expression and the
 `without waiting` suffix, and the compiler then refuses the program -
 `Expected a statement, got Without` - even on the SAME construct that
 compiles cleanly on one line, hand-verified repeatedly this session.
 Minimal repro (3 lines, no leaf involved):
   a number called r is reap process 5
   without waiting.
   Print r.
 fails; the same two lines joined ("...reap process 5 without waiting.")
 compiles and runs. `print ... without newline` - the OTHER `without`
 suffix in the language - tolerates the identical newline split fine, so
 this looks narrow to the reap-suffix's own grammar production rather
 than a general newline rule; LANGUAGE.md:226 states plainly that only a
 period closes a clause, which this appears to contradict. This is a
 layout-randomiser interaction (docs/ledger/PROCEDURE.md sect.5): the
 leaf below is complete, correct Vox and hand-verifies clean under both
 pinned compilers with ordinary formatting; it is left UNREGISTERED
 because the campaign's own layout pass can turn its output into this.
 PRC-55 (the one sub-claim here that does not need the suffix at all) is
 salvaged into 'gen leaf process reaped ordinary identifier' below
 instead, so it is not lost to this same hold.

 PRC-50, PRC-51, PRC-52, PRC-53, PRC-54, PRC-55: any reap form takes a
 `without waiting` suffix (WNOHANG) (LANGUAGE.md:4368-4375); the three
 outcomes are told apart by the return value alone - a child that
 finished returns its pid with the flag clear (LANGUAGE.md:4380), a
 child that exists but has not finished returns exactly 0 with the flag
 clear, NOT an error (LANGUAGE.md:4381-4382), and a genuine error (no
 such child) returns negative with the flag set (LANGUAGE.md:4383-4384).
 A poll that returns 0 reaps nothing and so does not disturb `the reaped
 status` (LANGUAGE.md:4386-4388). `without` is already reserved (the
 `print ... without newline` token) so the suffix cannot be confused
 with a call argument, and `waiting` remains an ordinary identifier
 everywhere it is not this exact suffix (LANGUAGE.md:4388-4391).

 SAFETY DEVIATION from the ledger's own retained probe
 (docs/ledger/probes/process-control/PRC-50.vox): that probe forces the
 "still running" observation with `Send signal 9` to the slow child
 before reaping it. This surface brief's standing policy restricts `Send
 signal` to signal 0 on a program's own live child ONLY, so this leaf
 gets the same observation a different way instead: the forked child
 `Wait`s a drawn interval before exiting, and the parent's FIRST poll
 (issued with no delay at all, right after the fork) is guaranteed to
 land before that interval elapses; a second poll, issued after the
 parent itself has waited comfortably longer than the child's interval,
 is then guaranteed to land after the child exited. Hand-verified this
 session, five back-to-back runs plus a deliberately-broken run, both
 pinned compilers, no flake: the child's own wait is drawn 100-399ms and
 the parent's second wait is that same draw plus a fixed 500ms margin,
 so the ordering is deterministic BY CONSTRUCTION, not by luck, even
 though the exact millisecond values vary every time this leaf is drawn
 - only the 500ms MARGIN is a fixed implementation detail (not a manual
 claim), the same way the retained probe's own fixed 300ms margin is
 documented as "not a claim about scheduling".

 The genuine-ECHILD probe (PRC-53) needs no live child at all - a
 reserved-invalid pid can never be this program's child - so it runs
 first, standalone, reusing the same "past any real pid_max" idiom
 PRC-47/48 above and PRC-49 (kind 265) already established. Every reap
 here also updates gen_process_has_reaped, the same shared per-program
 flag every other reaping kind in this file maintains, for PRC-58's
 sentinel claim elsewhere.)
